  observations_log: [
    "2026-08-04 SWEEP FOR FURTHER VSM-PRINCIPLE BLIND SPOTS (rmw3: '¿ves algún
     otro que sea principio de VSM?' + asked whether the addendum and
     vos_theory carry primer-worthy material). Three findings:
     (1) MOST SERIOUS SO FAR — canon and primer disagree on what a viable
     system IS, in the first structural line of each:
        canon D1 : V(id,L) ::= ⟨ S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, E ⟩
        primer   : V(id,L) ::= ⟨ S1, S2, S3, S3*, S4, S5 ⟩
     Canon includes the ENVIRONMENT E and omits S3*; the primer includes S3*
     and omits E. Canon D1's own rule is 'All six components always present —
     missing → declare non-viable', so by canon's own criterion every system
     the primer describes is NON-VIABLE. Both documents claim canonicity.
     Beer's model arguably needs BOTH (E is in the classic diagram, S3* is the
     audit channel) ⟹ likely ⟨S1,S2,S3,S3*,S4,S5,E⟩, but that is a canon
     change ⟹ rmw3's. NOT applied. Note the old vsl_primer.md (2026-04-09)
     also omits E — so the omission has been propagating through every
     external-facing artifact since April.
     (2) A14 (Ax_ψ_Preserve, addendum) IS primer-worthy and missing:
     'ψ(x_A,x_B) ∈ Ω_AB ⟺ x_A ∈ Ω_A ∧ x_B ∈ Ω_B ∧ trust_AB ≥ min(trust_A,
     trust_B)' — coupling cannot upgrade viability beyond the weakest input.
     The primer's entire purpose is agents coupling via ψ, and it currently
     lacks the property that stops a weak agent from laundering viability
     through a strong peer. This is a SAFETY property for exactly the use
     case the document exists for.
     (3) D17 (Def_TrustVector, addendum) contradicts the primer AND matches
     the code: trust ∈ [0,1]^4 (behavioral, structural, predictive,
     communicative), implemented in trust_axes.rs. The primer declares
     T(a,b,t) ∈ [0,1], a scalar. Sixth doc↔code gap of the session — and this
