     surfaces, symbiogenesis, meta-decision. vOS-specific dynamical theory,
     correctly outside a domain-agnostic language primer. Clean negative.",
    "2026-08-04 SECOND EXTERNAL REVIEW (Qwen Chat, relayed by rmw3). Four
     points, all four verified, and one led to a defect NEITHER reviewer saw:
     (1) ACCEPTED — `error_S2` used in trust{}'s audit formula, declared
     nowhere: 'technically violates your own closure rule'. Correct, and
     broader than the example: `reachability`, `stability` and `audit` were
     equally undeclared. Declared all four.
     THE REAL FINDING: this is the THIRD blind spot in verify_primer_closure.
     py found by an outsider. Axis 1 (glyphs) was mine; axis 2 (predicate
     arity) came from Kimi; axis 3 (bare ASCII terms) from Qwen. The script
     reported '✓ notation closed' before each. 'Closed' had quietly come to
     mean 'closed with respect to whatever I happened to check', which is the
     precise failure this file was written to prevent, committed by the file
     itself, three times.
     (2) ACCEPTED — no S2 channel in channels{}. A4 forbids sibling coupling
     and S2 is supposed to coordinate S1, so with only φ↑/φ↓/ψ declared, all
     sibling sync must route through S3 ⟹ bottleneck. The implementation
     already had it: projects/vsf-bbh/orchestrator/vsl_channel.py declares
     `lateral → S2 coordination (anti-collision between S1 units)`. Added as
     σ : S2 → S1(L), read-only for S1, with the explicit note that it does
     ¬violate A4 (it is parent→child, ¬child↔child).
     (3) FOUND WHILE CHECKING (2), MISSED BY BOTH REVIEWERS AND BY US: the
     primer NEVER MENTIONED THE ALGEDONIC CHANNEL. Zero occurrences. It
     appears 172 times in the Rust kernel, is cited in canon A6 ('bypasses
     tick latency — A6+algedonic Beer §8'), drives the entire S5 ledger, and
     is one of Beer's most important VSM constructs. The document whose whole
     purpose is onboarding an external agent to VSL omitted the emergency
     channel. Added as α : S1 → S5, binary, ∄ attenuation (A8 deliberately
     does not apply — that is the point of an algedonic signal).
     (4) A10 — Qwen argues urgency≥MEDIUM ⟹ S5 swamps S5 with operational
     triage, violating its insulation; proposes MEDIUM ⟹ S4. Cybernetically
     sound and Beer-faithful. But checked against the implementation first:
     algedonic.rs states 'Algedonic is BINARY: φ_algedonic ∈ {0,1}. No
     urgency levels in payload' and 'urgency by routing priority (NATS Core),
     ¬payload semantic'. So the deployed channel has no graded urgency at
     all, while the S5 ledger separately carries severity 8/9/10 and triages
     at ≥8. A10's LOW/MEDIUM split maps cleanly onto NEITHER. This is a real
     doc↔code divergence in CANON (not just the primer) and fixing it is a
     routing-design decision ⟹ rmw3's, NOT taken here. FIFTH instance of
     doc-behind-code this session.
     (5) A0 SCOPE — Qwen: forcing a JSON-parsing leaf agent to declare
     purpose ⊆ {human_liberation, ...} is semantically empty; proposes
     narrowing scope(A0,A0.1) to S5(V) ∪ V(id,0). The DEFECT IS REAL and
     sharper than stated: A0 as written is literally unsatisfiable for any
     concrete operational unit, since {parse_json} ⊄ {human_liberation,
     systemic_stability, dignity_of_life, harmonious_coexistence} as sets.
     But the PROPOSED FIX creates an exemption zone — a subsystem could act
     against A0 and claim A0 does not bind it, which is precisely the
     administrative_capture A0.1 exists to forbid. Alternatives that keep A0
     universal while fixing the set theory: (b) replace ⊆ with a
     compatibility relation ¬conflicts(purpose(V), A0.set); (c) purpose
     composition — purpose(V) ⊑ purpose(parent(V)) with only the root
     constrained by ⊆. CONSTITUTIONAL + axiom_Δ ∈ L3 ⟹ rmw3's decision, NOT
     taken here. Recorded with the analysis so the decision has the options
     and the trade-off in front of it.
     TOOLING DISCIPLINE APPLIED TO OURSELVES: axis 3 was built, measured at
     271 hits (all prose noise), tightened to 37 (still mostly field
     accesses, enum members, RHS labels, ∃-bound names) — and then NOT
     shipped as a gate. It is advisory-only behind --terms, with its unfixed
     noise classes named in the output. Regex cannot separate these; the
     project already has scripts/vsl_parser.py (v1.0 BNF, corpus 212/217)
     and axis 3 should be rebuilt on it. Shipping a 37-hit gate would have
     repeated exactly the mistake the canon-census review warned against
     four hours earlier, in the same session, in the same file family.",
    "2026-08-04 EXTERNAL REVIEW (Kimi K3, relayed by rmw3) + the B_exhausted/Ω
     tension it raised. Verdict on the review, point by point:
     ACCEPTED, both real and both mine: (a) `T` used with THREE arities —
     T(t) declared, T(V) and T(A,B) not; (b) `Q2` a dangling forward
     reference (Fierro's Q2, defined nowhere in the primer — unresolvable by
     the zero-context agent the primer exists for). I introduced Q2 the day
     before. WORSE THAN THE FINDINGS THEMSELVES: verify_primer_closure.py
     reported '✓ notation closed' straight through both, because it compared
     GLYPHS only. A checker that passes a document containing undefined
     predicates is worse than no checker — it converts an open question into
     false confidence, which is precisely the failure mode this session keeps
     finding, now inside the verification tool built to prevent it. Fixed:
     added an arity axis (every predicate must be used with a declared
     arity), negative-tested. The new axis immediately found a FOURTH defect
     neither review caught — the primer declares V(id, L) but uses V(L)
     throughout.
     ACCEPTED DIAGNOSIS, REJECTED CURE: `additive` as a measure name is
     circular (named after the operator it justifies) — correct. But the
     proposed replacement (log|states|) would assert something VSF does not
     compute and would manufacture fresh doc-vs-code drift. Named instead by
     what the kernel actually does: measure(Var) = Σ_i |Δx_i| (total
     variation, L1, over independent axes) — additive by the norm's own
     definition. Added the caveat the critique surfaces indirectly: it is a
     perturbation-magnitude proxy, ¬a state count, so VSF's A6 check is an
     attenuation reading, ¬a cardinality one.
     OUT OF SCOPE (category error, stated as such rather than conceded):
     A0-without-oracle, S3*-without-authentication, Sybil resistance are
     operational-security critiques of a document whose scope{} explicitly
     excludes deployment; and 'A0 is not verifiable' is true BY
     CONSTRUCTION — the primer itself states ¬provable(A0,A0.1,A1..A11).
     Those belong to the operational layer that already exists separately
     (covenant_ops.vsm, vsl_regulation_layer_v1.vsm). The review's scorecard
     merges the two layers and therefore concludes 'formally beautiful, not
     operationally safe' about a document that never claimed the second.
     RESOLVED — the B_exhausted/Ω tension (the review's one remaining
     IN-SCOPE structural point, and a genuine defect of Claude's 08-04
     grounding): A9 offers `degrade` as the alternative to `abort`, i.e. as
     survival, while degrade ⊃ {skip(S1),...}; if skipping S1 leaves Ω, a
     degrading system is simultaneously surviving and non-viable. Root cause
     was Claude defining viable(V) ⟺ state(V) ∈ Ω(V) as BINARY without
     checking it against A9. The implementation already had the answer:
     calibration.rs defines in_precautionary_band(stasis_cos) =
     'still viable (≥ VIABLE_THRESHOLD 0.70) but below the PSI precautionary
     threshold (THETA_PSI 0.79)' — an explicitly named band INSIDE Ω,
     adjacent to ∂Ω, plus BASIN_FLOOR 0.875 as the observed-resilience
     envelope. So Ω is three-banded, not binary, and `degrade` lives in the
     precautionary band: it is the control action that HOLDS the trajectory
     inside Ω while ∂Ω is near, not a partial exit from it. Far from
     contradicting viability, degrade is what preserves it — which is also
     what canon A9c already said ('degrade: ... preserve(governance)').
     Primer updated: Ω declared ¬binary with the three-band mapping, and A9
     given the explicit link `degrade ⟹ viable(V)`.
     PATTERN WORTH NOTING: for the third time this session the resolution
     was already in the implementation and absent from the doc (Q-vsf-11 →
     signal_router.py; ⊕ → operational.rs; Ω-bands → calibration.rs). The
     doc layer has been consistently behind the code layer, never ahead.",
    "2026-08-04 REVIEW OF STAGE 1 BEFORE BUILDING STAGE 2 (rmw3: 'hagamos
     una revision antes de paso 2'). Correct call — the review changes what
     stage 2 should be.
     VERDICT: ~2 real findings out of ~20 flagged. Precision ≈ 10%.
     REAL (both invisible to two days of careful manual reading):
       - LAMBDA_MAX declared twice, same crate, unrelated meanings
         (trust_axes.rs:27 = 0.20 trust-decay clamp ; calibration.rs:53 =
         -0.01005 largest Lyapunov exponent). Live footgun. NOT fixed here:
         renaming live Rust ⟹ rebuild+redeploy, same class as task #31 —
         queued, not applied unilaterally.
       - duplicate primer (vsl_primer.md), independently rediscovered.
     FALSE POSITIVES, each with its cause identified (this is the valuable
     part of the review — every one is a stage-2 filter):
       - cfg-gated const pairs: COVENANT_DEGRADED_THRESHOLD (0.80/1.0),
         HALT_AFTER_DEGRADED (45/5), ESCALATION_GRACE_TICKS (90/3) are all
         #[cfg(feature=\"exp_s5_01\")] production/experiment pairs, correctly
         written and documented. The census cannot see cfg gates.
       - unit mismatch: ATTEN_STEP doc '20%' vs code 0.20 — SAME value,
         different notation.
       - test fixtures in comments: D_KY 3.0/3.5 are values inside a unit
         test's comments, not constants at all.
       - regex over-capture: SAFETY_TEMP is 70.0 in BOTH sites; the 80/85
         came from neighbouring text.
       - shell branch variables: APPLY/EXECUTE/FIRED/EXIT 0-vs-1.
       - label ≠ concept: Ax_S5CautionVeto flagged 'no code trace', but it
         IS implemented as `caution_mode`.
       - ALIAS PROBLEM (the most important one found): the same axiom
         carries BOTH a numeric ID and a semantic name — A13 ≡
         Ax_RecurrentSet, A12 ≡ Ax_HardwareCeiling — and the census treats
         them as unrelated identifiers. lyapunov.rs:4 cites
         'dynamics_choice.vsm §T2 (Ax_RecurrentSet)', so A13 IS implemented
         and IS cited, yet it was reported as untraced.
       - restatement ≠ contradiction: A5 'in 16 docs' is mostly legitimate
         restatement; co-occurrence is not conflict.
     INCONCLUSIVE, still worth chasing: A9 (B_exhausted ⟹ degrade ∨ abort)
     and A11 (S4_active ⟺ drift > ε) produced no concept-level hits under
     obvious alternative names either. May be genuinely unimplemented.
     RECOMMENDATION — stage 2 is NOT new rules, it is PRECISION on the
     existing ones: (1) an alias table (numeric ID ↔ Ax_/Def_ name) without
     which query[3] is unreliable; (2) cfg/conditional-compilation
     awareness; (3) strip comments and test blocks before extracting
     constants; (4) unit normalization (% vs fraction); (5) reframe query[2]
     — the valuable signal is same-name-DIFFERENT-SEMANTICS (a namespace
     collision, which is what LAMBDA_MAX is), not same-name-different-value,
     which is usually legitimate. Only after precision is up should new
     rules (statement diffing, RESOLVED-without-citation, duplicate purpose)
     be added, and only then wired into pre-commit.
     WHY THIS ORDER MATTERS HERE SPECIFICALLY: this project already has a
     dated instance of a real alert being drowned by tolerated noise
     (project_sensor_omega_integration: a genuine signal lost among 7 weeks
     of pct-bug false positives) and a standing rule against blunting the
     audit (feedback_dont_blunt_the_audit). A 10%-precision gate wired into
     pre-commit would be trained-to-ignore within a week and would then be
     WORSE than no gate — it would manufacture exactly the tolerated-noise
     condition the certification charter exists to prevent.",
    "2026-08-04 STAGE 1 OF THE PIPELINE BUILT (rmw3 approved): scripts/docs/
     canon_census.py. Indexes docs AND code into ONE sqlite (docs/
     .canon_census.db, gitignored, regenerated never hand-edited) so that
     'do these two agree?' becomes a query. Scanned 185 docs + 206 code
     files ⟹ 1755 identifier occurrences (220 definitions), 697 constants,
     2397 refs, 127 status markers. Every conflict rule it runs is derived
     from a real failure this session, not invented.
     FOUND ON ITS FIRST RUN, previously unknown to anyone:
     LAMBDA_MAX is declared TWICE as pub const in the same crate with
     unrelated meanings — trust_axes.rs:27 = 0.20 (trust-decay clamp
     ceiling) and calibration.rs:53 = -0.01005 (largest Lyapunov exponent,
     EXP-LY-01 Benettin). Exactly the A0/A4/A12 collision pattern, but in
     CONSTANTS, in live Rust, and no human or agent had ever surfaced it.
     A module importing both gets whichever is in scope. This is the single
     best argument for the pipeline: it found in one run a class of defect
     that two days of careful manual reading did not.
     ALSO: the census INDEPENDENTLY rediscovered the duplicate-primer
     finding — docs/spec/vsl_primer.md appears beside vsl_core_boot_primer_
     v1.vsm and vsm_notation_spec.md as a third definer of A1,A2,A3,A4,A6,
     A7,A8,A9,A11. Mechanical confirmation of what had taken 4 months and a
     direct question from rmw3 to notice.
     HONEST NOISE ASSESSMENT (this is a LEAD GENERATOR, not a verdict —
     stage 2 must filter, and the limits are real):
       - query[2] false positives: shell variables assigned different values
         in different branches of the SAME file (APPLY 0/1, EXECUTE 0/1,
         FIRED 0/1, EXIT 0/124). Filter: same-file branch assignment.
       - query[3] 'no code trace' means NO LABEL MATCH, NOT 'unenforced'.
         Demonstrated live: it flagged Ax_S5CautionVeto as having no code
         trace; the concept IS implemented, under the name `caution_mode`
         (vsf_algedonic_subscriber.py, s5_covenant.rs). So the finding is
         real but it is a TRACEABILITY gap (axiom ↔ enforcement site never
         declared — Ax_TraceabilityClosure, task #3), not an absence.
         Claude initially mislabeled this output as 'not implemented' and
         the very next command disproved it — the tool's own discipline
         caught the tool's user.
       - query[1] conflates 'restated' with 'contradicted'. A5 shows 16
         docs; most are legitimate restatements. Stage 2 needs statement
         COMPARISON (normalized text diff), not just co-occurrence.
     NEXT (stage 2, not built): filters above + statement-diff for real
     contradiction + doc-vs-code constant comparison as a first-class rule
     + 'RESOLVED/DONE without a code or experiment citation' + duplicate
     declared-purpose detection. Stage 3 = wire into pre-commit like the
     existing guards.",
    "2026-08-04 (rmw3: 'pudiste revisar otros documentos de docs/spec /theory
