    "2026-08-04 (2nd coherence pass, rmw3: 'ojo en ley de ashby y coherencia
     con fierro'). Three findings, one of them a REAL MATHEMATICAL ERROR:
     (1) BUG, FIXED — the primer formalized A8's absorption corollary as
     `r=0.9 ⟹ |S(L+2)|/|S(L)| ≈ 0.01`. That ratio is INVERTED: with
     |S(L)| ≤ |S(L+1)|·(1−r) applied twice, |S(L)|/|S(L+2)| = 0.01, so the
     stated ratio is actually 100 — wrong by 10^4. Canon's PROSE gloss is
     correct ('grandparent sees ~1% of grandchild signals'); the primer's
     symbolic rendering of that same sentence flipped it. Arithmetic
     re-derived numerically before asserting. Predates the notation work —
     it was in the primer from its first draft. Note what this says about
     the compression claim: prose-correct ⟹̸ formalization-correct, and
     only the formal version is machine-checkable. Fixed to
     |S(L)|/|S(L+2)| ≈ 0.01.
     (2) GAP, FIXED — `viable` was ungrounded. The primer's very first line
     defines VSL over `V := viable_system(...)` and the file never said what
     viable MEANS; the notation-closure work then compounded it by
     introducing `infeasible(V) := viable(V) = ⊥` on top of the undefined
     predicate. The glyph checker cannot catch this class (it checks
     symbols, not predicates) — it took rmw3's Fierro question to surface
     it. Grounded abstractly as `viable(V) ⟺ state(V) ∈ Ω(V)`, with Ω added
     to primitives and its concrete form explicitly left out of the primer
     (VSF instantiates Ω as a convex polytope per Fierro Q1; a conforming
     system need only expose membership + margin to ∂Ω). This keeps the
     primer domain-agnostic while making the term checkable.
     (3) OPEN, NOT DECIDED — `⊕` (A6, Ashby) is UNDERDETERMINED, and worse
     than CT7 recorded. canon Part I defines it only as 'Variety combination
     (Ashby)', never as an operation. Its value depends on an undeclared
     choice: measure(Var)=|states| ⟹ ⊕=· ; measure(Var)=log|states| ⟹ ⊕=+
     (Ashby's own measure was logarithmic). Until measure(Var) is fixed, A6
     is not mechanically checkable — an axiom that cannot be evaluated.
     Fixing it is axiom_Δ ∈ L3 ⟹ human approval required by A5 itself, so
     it is FLAGGED IN THE PRIMER (notation.OPEN(⊕)) and NOT chosen here.
     rmw3's decision.
     (3b) CT7 NEEDS REFRAMING (task #10). CT7 currently reads as
     'recursive_vsm_theory.md §6 wrongly writes Var(S3)·Var(S2) instead of
     canon's ⊕ operator — mechanical fix'. That framing is backwards: the
     theory doc's `·` is the only place in the whole repo that COMMITS to
     what the operation is; canon's `⊕` is the vacuous one. Applying CT7 as
     written would DELETE the project's only statement of Ashby's
     combination rule and replace it with an undefined glyph — a net loss of
     information dressed as a consistency fix. CT7 must wait on the
     measure(Var) decision in (3): if measure(Var)=|states| then the theory
     doc was right all along and canon should adopt `·`; if logarithmic,
     both change. Also found while checking: `⊕` is overloaded 3 ways in
     canon, not 1 — variety combination (line 84), AXIOM conjunction
     ('Corollary 2 (A5⊕A2)', line 78), and generic quantity combination
     (resistance line 274, trust_score line 432). Same disease as ⟹/⊥.
     (4) COHERENCE WITH FIERRO — verified, no conflict, one confusion
     pre-empted. Fierro Q1/Q2/Q3 (Ω convex, Lipschitz L<1, σ_max) live in
     the nucleus (omega_validator.rs, lyapunov.rs, vr_complex.rs +
     calibration.rs) per docs/papers/attractor_convergence/Revisiones/
     fierro_bridge_to_operations.vsm. Correctly ABSENT from the primer as
     concrete values (implementation calibration, excluded by scope) — but
     A8's `(1−r_L) < 1` and Q2's `L < 1` are both contraction conditions in
     DIFFERENT spaces (signal counts per level vs state trajectory in Ω),
     structurally analogous and numerically unrelated. An external agent
     reading primer + paper together could easily conflate them, so the
     primer's foundation{} now states the distinction explicitly.",
